#!/usr/bin/env python3
#
# romwbw-batch - boot CP/M 2.2 on the emulator, run a list of commands with
# nobody at the keyboard, and take the files back out.
#
#   romwbw-batch -a hello.asm -c "ASM HELLO" -c "LOAD HELLO" -g HELLO.COM
#
# What it does, in order:
#
#   1. copies the CP/M 2.2 system disk (hd1k_cpm22, from `romwbw-get`) to a
#      scratch image - the cached original is read-only and stays that way;
#   2. adds the host files you name to A:, and to B: if you ask for B: (see
#      DRIVES below) - a file named like one of the system disk's own
#      replaces it in the scratch copy: `-t HELLO.ASM` is yours, not
#      RomWBW's sample;
#   3. writes the commands as A:$$$.SUB, in the layout DRI's SUBMIT.COM writes,
#      so the CCP runs them at boot exactly as if SUBMIT had been typed;
#   4. boots with stdin on /dev/null, so the run ends by itself: when the last
#      command is done the CCP asks the console for a line, finds end of input,
#      and the emulator winds down (see "EXIT" below);
#   5. checks that the batch reached its end, and extracts the files you name
#      - with a warning for any from A: that is still, byte for byte, the
#      system disk's own, which a failed rebuild of it leaves there.
#
# WHY $$$.SUB AND NOT PIPED STDIN.  Piped stdin works for a command or two and
# then loses keystrokes, for reasons that are the guest's and not the
# emulator's: a real keyboard typed ahead loses them the same way.  Every CP/M
# 2.2 BDOS console write polls CONST for ^S, a BDOS error waits for "any key",
# DDT and PIP abort on a key.  (RomWBW's boot loader, which read and dropped
# everything up to the first CR while it counted down to autoboot, no longer
# sees piped input but a leading Esc: the emulator holds it until the guest
# first asks for a key, or for a while after the loader hands over.)  A
# submit file is read by the CCP one record per command and never races
# anything - and a key waiting when the CCP reads a record would abort it, as
# a key pressed during SUBMIT does.
#
# EXIT.  The emulator stops on its own when stdin is at end of file and the
# guest either reads the console or sits polling it.  That is what happens after
# the last command, and also what happens when a command fails in a way that
# waits for a key.  So "the emulator exited" does not mean "the batch finished";
# the batch ends with `TYPE ROMWBW.END`, a file this tool adds holding a string
# it made up, and the run counts as complete only if that string reached the
# console.  An error that aborts a submit file (the CCP's "?" or ISX's) deletes
# $$$.SUB, so the sentinel never prints and the tool says where it stopped.
#
# DRIVES.  A: is slice 0 of the system disk.  B: on a RomWBW disk boot is the
# RAM disk (MD0), which starts empty and dies with the run; files you add to B:
# go instead to a second hd1k image attached as --disk1, and the batch's first
# command is `ASSIGN B:=HDSK1:0,G:=MD0:0`, which swaps it into B:.  (ASSIGN
# refuses a map in which two letters name one filesystem, hence the swap rather
# than a plain assignment.)  That is what lets DRI's ISX recipes, which keep
# the tools on :F1:, run unchanged.
#
# ISX (--isx).  DRI's "ISIS-II INTERFACE VERS 1.4" runs Intel's ISIS-II tools
# under CP/M 2.2.  It carries its own BDOS and calls the BIOS directly, and it
# needs things from this machine that RomWBW does not give it by default;
# docs/ISX.md has the full account.  Two ways to run it:
#
#   --isx=compact (the default).  ISX runs on tools/isxbios.asm, a 400-byte
#     CP/M 2.2 BIOS at F600H that talks to HBIOS directly, installed by a few
#     bytes this tool puts in front of ISX.COM.  ISIS programs get memory up
#     to F5FFH - enough for PL/M-80 to compile every MP/M II utility, which it
#     is not at E5FFH.  Its WBOOT asks HBIOS for a reset, so leaving ISX (DRI's
#     `CPM` jumps through 0001H) reboots CP/M from the same disk, and the CCP
#     carries on with $$$.SUB.  A reboot rebuilds the CBIOS drive map, so the
#     tool re-issues the B: swap after every `CPM`.  :F0: and :F1: are --disk0
#     and --disk1, straight to HBIOS, and B: is attached even when nothing is
#     added to it, because ISIS programs put their work files on :F1:.
#
#   --isx=cbios.  ISX runs on RomWBW's own CBIOS, and ISIS programs stop at
#     E5FFH.  ISX's `CPM` jumps to the BIOS warm boot expecting the BDOS to be
#     reloaded - ISX lets ISIS programs overwrite it - and RomWBW's warm boot
#     deliberately reloads only the CCP (cbios.asm: "WE DON'T WANT TO RELOAD
#     BDOS"), so the machine would hang.  The tool installs its own `CPM`, an
#     ISIS module that copies the BDOS back from the system tracks, restores
#     the BIOS cold-boot vector ISX patched, and then warm boots.  ISX also
#     copies the MDS monitor vector to F800H-F87FH, inside the CBIOS's free
#     heap; the tool checks after the run that the heap left it free.
#
# Either way, exact file lengths: ISX keeps the number of UNUSED bytes in a
# file's last record in directory byte 13 (S1).  DRI's SETEOF.COM sets it
# through BDOS close, which a stock CP/M 2.2 BDOS never copies to the
# directory, so on this machine SETEOF does nothing and PL/M-80 reads the ^Z
# padding as "UNPRINTABLE ASCII CHARACTER" errors.  --isx (or --exact) writes
# S1 when adding a file and honours it when extracting one.
#
# The files it needs: cpm_disk.py (cpmemu's disk tool - $CPM_DISK, then a
# sister ../cpmemu checkout, then a `cpm_disk` on PATH), the romwbw_emu binary
# ($ROMWBW_EMU, ../src/romwbw_emu, then PATH) and, unless --rom and --disk name
# files, tools/romwbw-get to find the ROM and the system disk.
#
# LIMITS.  --timeout kills a run after that many seconds of wall clock, and the
# emulator stops one after --max-instructions Z80 instructions - its own
# default when stdin is not a terminal, which in a batch it never is, is ten
# billion, some seven minutes at full speed - and says so, with exit status
# 124.  Either way the batch did not complete, and the tool says which
# stopped it.
#
# Exit status: 0 the batch completed and every requested file was extracted;
# 1 it did not complete, or a requested file is missing; 2 something it needs
# is missing (emulator, ROM, disk, cpm_disk.py); 64 usage, a file to add that
# is not there among it; 130 interrupted.  The arguments are checked before
# anything is looked for.
# The scratch directory is removed on every exit but --work.

